    kind: invariant  # b915
    title: "ssl.SSLObject.read/write/do_handshake declared net_recv/net_send but the class does no network I/O — three rows mint untrusted_input from nowhere"  # b915
    status: violated  # b915
    priority: 2  # b915
    parent:  # b915
    tags:  # b915
    - taint  # b915
    - catalogue  # b915
    - analysis_quality  # b915
    isbefore: []  # b915
    duplicate_of: []  # b915
    not_duplicate_of: []  # b915
    pr_ref:  # b915
    description: ""  # b915
    fields:  # b915
      statement: "The python catalogue rows for ssl.SSLObject.read (net_recv), ssl.SSLObject.write (net_send) and ssl.SSLObject.do_handshake (net_send) assert a network crossing at a class that performs none. CPython ssl.py:784-797 states SSLObject does not provide any network IO itself and explicitly lacks any form of network IO including methods such as recv and send; all I/O is performed by the caller through memory BIOs. A source row that mints untrusted_input where no far-side data enters is a false-positive generator."  # b915
      root_cause: "Kind asserted by name. SSLObject methods are spelled read/write/do_handshake, matching the net_recv/net_send naming convention used for SSLSocket whose methods DO cross the network. The catalogue did not distinguish the BIO-backed class from the socket-backed one."  # b915
