- op: discuss  # bcc2
  at: '2026-09-21T09:14:18Z'  # bcc2
  by: agent  # bcc2
  actor: jgstern_agent  # bcc2
  clock: 3  # bcc2
  nonce: bcc2  # bcc2
  message: "STALENESS AUDIT 2026-09-21 (owner-requested). VERDICT: NOT STALE. All three rows are still in the catalogue, and the CPython premise is confirmed against the interpreter on this machine rather than against the citation.\n\nTHE ROWS, READ TODAY in packages/hypergumbo-core/src/hypergumbo_core/io_primitives/python.yaml:\n  net_send  line 555-556   - module: ssl.SSLObject   methods: [write, do_handshake]\n  net_recv  line 602-603   - module: ssl.SSLObject   methods: [read]\nThree method entries across two boundaries, exactly as filed. Nothing has touched them.\n\nTHE PREMISE, RE-VERIFIED FROM SOURCE -- and deliberately not from the filed citation, because a line reference is a claim. Read out of the LOCAL stdlib (CPython 3.12.3, inspect.getsource(ssl)), the class docstring says:\n\n  \"This object captures the state of an SSL connection but does not provide\n   any network IO itself. IO needs to be performed through separate \"BIO\"\n   objects which are OpenSSL's IO abstraction layer.\"\n\n  \"When compared to ``SSLSocket``, this object lacks the following features:\n    * Any form of network IO, including methods such as ``recv`` and ``send``.\"\n\nThat is the row's statement in CPython's own words, including the explicit denial of recv and send. The filing cited ssl.py:784-797; the text is present in 3.12.3 wherever the line numbers now fall, so the claim holds independently of the line reference.\n\nWHY IT STILL MATTERS AND IS NOT COSMETIC. These are SOURCE rows on net_recv: `ssl.SSLObject.read` mints untrusted_input. A source row that mints untrusted_input where no far-side data enters is a false-positive generator, and it is the same defect class as INV-nular (primitive kind asserted by name) -- which was closed `satisfied` while these three survived, because the sweep that found them (WI-vafad T5, 2026-09-11) filed a candidate list rather than edits, per its own rubric R4.\n\nTHE CONTRAST IS IN THE SAME FILE, two lines up: `ssl.SSLSocket` carries [send, sendall, sendto, sendmsg, write, connect, connect_ex, do_handshake] on net_send and [recv, recvfrom, recv_into, recvfrom_into, recvmsg, recvmsg_into, read, accept] on net_recv, and those ARE socket-backed and DO cross. The catalogue distinguishes the two classes by name and not by what they do, which is precisely the root_cause as filed.\n\nNOT ASSERTED: that the fix is free. The root_cause note says the fix pays ADR-0049 ruling 3 separately, and I have not re-examined that. STATUS STAYS `violated`."  # bcc2
- op: discuss  # 5d9a
  at: '2026-09-21T09:19:57Z'  # 5d9a
  by: agent  # 5d9a
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 5d9a
  clock: 4  # 5d9a
  nonce: 5d9a  # 5d9a
  message: "THE ADR-0049 RULING 3 LICENCE, WORKED BEFORE CODING (2026-09-21). The root_cause says the fix \"pays ADR-0049 ruling 3 separately\". Here is what that costs, because the answer decides whether these rows are REMOVED or RETAGGED.\n\nRULING 3 AS WRITTEN: \"Removing or retagging a deferred-crossing row is licensed only once EITHER the arrival scope is represented as a source, OR the disclosure boundary is in place. Both halves land in the same change... Test removal at the finding level, not the row level.\"\n\nFIRST, A SCOPE QUESTION THAT MATTERS: these are not deferred-crossing rows. Ruling 4's table cuts the family by mechanism -- Setup, Blocking serve loop, Per-connection accept, Route-registration DSL, Handle constructor, Lazy/unexecuted, Callback-delivered -- and `SSLObject.read` is none of them. A deferred crossing is one that happens LATER; this is a row asserting a crossing that happens NOWHERE in the class. So Ruling 3 does not bind here by its own terms.\n\nBUT ITS TEST IS THE RIGHT TEST ANYWAY, and I intend to pay it rather than argue my way out of it, because the substantive risk it guards against is exactly the one here: deleting a row that was load-bearing for a real finding. WI-lunav is the precedent -- dropping `newManager`, a row that \"transmits nothing\", collapsed a real violated(3) to inconclusive(0).\n\nTHE ARRIVAL SCOPE IS REPRESENTED, and that is the licence. In the BIO model the caller does the network I/O itself:\n\n    data = sock.recv(4096)        <- socket.socket.recv, CATALOGUED net_recv\n    incoming.write(data)          <- memory BIO, in-process\n    plaintext = sslobj.read()     <- SSLObject.read, THE ROW IN QUESTION\n    ...\n    sock.send(outgoing.read())    <- socket.socket.send, CATALOGUED net_send\n\nThe far-side bytes enter at `sock.recv` and leave at `sock.send`, both of which are already source and sink rows. Removing the SSLObject rows therefore relocates nothing and deletes no crossing -- it stops DOUBLE-MINTING a crossing the socket rows already represent, and stops minting one at all in the case the class exists for: a BIO fed from something that is not a socket.\n\nWHAT I WILL SHIP AS THE PROOF, at the finding level as the ruling requires, not at the row level: a fixture exercising the wrap_bio idiom, asserting that untrusted_input is still minted -- rooted at the socket read -- after the three rows are gone. If that assertion fails, the rows were load-bearing and removal is refused.\n\nNOT ASSERTED YET: that removal is the right disposition for all three. `write` and `do_handshake` need the same proof on the send side and get it in the same change, or they do not move."  # 5d9a
